
:ST

# OPENING - person_description's introducing vocative. Renders the description
# subject's name as a standalone phrase ("Thomas Jones.") so the body's first
# clause can pronominalise from the very next sentence. The MC mirrors the
# generic-possessive template's predicate-splice shape (so the matcher's
# proven path applies); the NL emits only the name. Tagged `:: intro` - the
# slot-bound intent that only `select opening:` requests, guaranteeing this
# template wins the match against the structurally-identical possessive form
# below. The discourse planner routes only the description subject's own
# name belief into the opening slot, so this never fires for other objects.
"$pname(PNAME)"
{ $sub_np(NP) $pname_rel(PASSIVE_PNAME_REL) $pname(PNAME) }
:: intro
.

"I don't know"
@unknown
.

"I'm not sure"
@unknown%
.

"Yes"
@true
.

"No"
@false
.

# Formulaic phrases
"$form(FORM)"
$form(FORM)
.


# Command-statements

"tell me about $obj(Q_NP)"
{ ?_I /pres goal { ?_you /pres tell_about $obj(Q_NP) ?_I } }
.

"I want you to tell me about $obj(Q_NP)"
{ ?_I /pres goal { ?_you /pres tell_about $obj(Q_NP) ?_I } }
.

# Kind-quantified content request: "tell me about Olivia's relationships",
# "tell me about her interests", "tell me about his children". The
# $state_kind hole uses KIND_PLUR (Kind.mgr) - PLURAL-ONLY surface forms,
# strict, no auto-stripping. Singular kinship like "tell me about your
# brother" deliberately does NOT match this template; it falls through to
# the "tell me about $obj(Q_NP)" path above. Q_NP resolves the referent
# any-tense + /known - (o /known {?_you /ever brother @o}) - so a DECEASED
# relative (kinship belief ends at death) still resolves instead of failing a
# present-tense recall, and an unknown referent yields @unknown rather than an
# invented object. Plural surface forms
# ("brothers", "interests") match here and the (every {?_you brother ? ?})
# search enumerates all matching beliefs. KIND_PLUR's MC binding is a kind
# atom [k <singular>] so the runtime isa expansion picks up child kinds for
# collective queries like "relationships".
"tell me about $obj(NP) ++'s $state_kind(KIND_PLUR)"
{ ?_I /pres goal { ?_you /pres tell_beliefs { $obj(NP) $state_kind(KIND_PLUR) ? ? } ?_I } }
.

# Possessive-pronoun shortcut: "tell me about your relationships" - no
# trailing 's needed because "your" / "my" / "his" / "her" already encode
# the possessive. NPC describes its own beliefs (subject = ?_you = @self
# in the receiving rule), which avoids any cross-mind subject resolution.
"tell me about your $state_kind(KIND_PLUR)"
{ ?_I /pres goal { ?_you /pres tell_beliefs { ?_you $state_kind(KIND_PLUR) ? ? } ?_I } }
.

"tell me about my $state_kind(KIND_PLUR)"
{ ?_I /pres goal { ?_you /pres tell_beliefs { ?_I $state_kind(KIND_PLUR) ? ? } ?_I } }
.


# BARE-IS ATTRIBUTE STATEMENTS
# e.g. "john is 56", "john is english", "john is a physician".
# Each template names its specific label (age / nationality / job). The
# $kind(KIND|/isa|<ancestor>) qualifier restricts the value to that label's
# kind, so the three templates never poach each other's values and so they
# are not confused with the generic "X is <kind>" -> isa STATE rule below.
# Note: non-terminal qualifiers are pipe-separated with NO spaces - the
# phrase tokenizer splits on whitespace before parsing the (...) payload.
# Placed before the possessive and generic-state templates so they take
# priority in both directions (first-match-wins, see _translate).

"$sub_np(NP) $verb(VERB|be) $num(NUM)"
{ $sub_np(NP) /ever age $num(NUM) }
.

# Terse age - clipped, comma-attached, no copula. The clerk-ledger voice: a
# fact dropped on the floor without ceremony. Picked by the .mph age select
# when cog:abstract holds (the speaker compresses to the claim). /ever so a
# deceased subject's age renders the same clipped form (tense is carried by
# the surrounding clauses, not the bare number).
" $sub_np(NP) ++, $num(NUM) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /ever age $num(NUM) }
:: terse curt
.

# Elaborated age - the schooled voice. Picked by the .mph age select when
# schooling:high holds; the speaker reaches for the more formal periphrasis
# instead of the bare copula. Same belief shape - parse accepts either.
" $sub_np(NP) has attained the age of $num(NUM) years "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres age $num(NUM) }
:: elaborated
.

"$sub_np(NP) $verb(VERB|be) $kind(KIND|/isa|nationality)"
{ $sub_np(NP) /ever nationality $kind(KIND|/isa|nationality) }
.

# class_situation is a kind-valued state ([k lower] / [k middle] / [k upper]);
# the trailing "-class" suffix reads naturally for all three values.
"$sub_np(NP) $verb(VERB|be) $kind(KIND|/isa|class_situation) ++-class"
{ $sub_np(NP) /ever class_situation $kind(KIND|/isa|class_situation) }
.

# Occupation - several phrasings of the same belief shape. "works as a X" is
# the longer / more conversational default; "is a X" is the curt / terse form;
# "makes his living as a X" is the affectionate / approving form, picked when
# the speaker holds the subject in regard. All materialize the same belief, so
# parsing accepts any of them.
"$sub_np(NP) $verb(VERB|work) as a $kind(KIND|/isa|job)"
{ $sub_np(NP) /ever job $kind(KIND|/isa|job) }
.

"$sub_np(NP) $verb(VERB|be) a $kind(KIND|/isa|job)"
{ $sub_np(NP) /ever job $kind(KIND|/isa|job) }
:: curt terse
.

# Warm - the speaker approves of the subject's livelihood; "makes his living"
# casts honest labour as a virtue. The 1897-narrator turn of phrase.
"$sub_np(NP) makes his living as a $kind(KIND|/isa|job)"
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres job $kind(KIND|/isa|job) }
:: warm
.

# Elaborated - the schooled voice. "By profession" carries the periphrastic
# formality the educated speaker reaches for. Picked by the .mph occupation
# select when schooling:high holds.
"$sub_np(NP) is by profession a $kind(KIND|/isa|job)"
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres job $kind(KIND|/isa|job) }
:: elaborated
.

# Flowery - the romantic-narrator's reach. "Plies the trade of" is 1897 high
# style for occupation - the kind of phrasing a well-read speaker uses without
# irony. Same belief shape as the neutral form.
"$sub_np(NP) plies the trade of a $kind(KIND|/isa|job)"
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres job $kind(KIND|/isa|job) }
:: flowery
.


# THIRD-PARTY KINSHIP / RELATION STATEMENTS.
# e.g. "John's father is Maurice", "John's spouse is Mary".
#
# These are the explicit per-label forms the description renderer relies on.
# A generic "$sub_np ++'s $passive_rel(PASSIVE_REL) $np" template exists below
# but the structural realize's predicate-fragment path rejects PASSIVE_REL's
# non-terminal label position (_realize_predicate in nat_lang_realize.cc), so
# these label-literal templates carry the description corpus. Mirrors the
# bare-is statements (age / nationality / job) above.

# Phase 8.2 - one template covers both tenses. $verb(VERB|be) emits "is" /
# "was" by reading the surrounding belief's tense at realize; at parse the
# matcher deconjugates the input and replaces the MC's /ever placeholder
# with the recovered tense before committing.
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s mother $verb(VERB|be) $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /ever mother $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s father $verb(VERB|be) $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /ever father $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s parent is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres parent $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s sibling is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres sibling $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s brother is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres brother $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s sister is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres sister $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s child is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres child $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s spouse is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres spouse $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s fiancee is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres fiancee $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s friend is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres friend $value(NP) } .
" $sub_np(NP) ++'s neighbour is $value(NP) "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres neighbour $value(NP) } .

# Terse / curt variants - fronted-name, dropped copula. The clerkly form a
# brisk speaker reaches for when reciting a kinship roster: "Emily, Charlotte's
# mother." Same belief shape as the neutral form above; parse accepts either.
# The .mph relations select picks these when cog:abstract or intent:terse holds.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s mother "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres mother $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s father "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres father $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s parent "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres parent $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s sibling "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres sibling $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s brother "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres brother $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s sister "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres sister $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s child "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres child $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s spouse "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres spouse $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s fiancee "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres fiancee $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s friend "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres friend $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.
" $value(NP) ++, $sub_np(NP) ++'s neighbour "
{ $sub_np(NP) /pres neighbour $value(NP) }
:: terse curt
.

